o American cheese. It was developed in 1918 by Emil Frey of the Monroe Cheese Company in Monroe, New York. In 1923, The Velveeta Cheese Company was incorporated as a separate company. In 1925, it advertised two varieties, Swiss and American. The firm was purchased by Kraft Foods Inc. in 1927."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-velveeta"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Velveeta"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["Mexican cuisine","cuisine/mexican-cuisine",[["Chile con queso","dish/chile-con-queso"]]],["Tex-Mex cuisine","cuisine/tex-mex-cuisine",[["Chile con queso","dish/chile-con-queso"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Cheese","ingredient/cheese"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Cream","ingredient/cream"],["Cream cheese","ingredient/cream-cheese"],["Monterey Jack","ingredient/monterey-jack"],["Processed cheese","ingredient/processed-cheese"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/6/69/Velveeta_Cheese.JPG/500px-Velveeta_Cheese.JPG","w":500,"h":389,"by":"PeRshGo","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Velveeta_Cheese.JPG"}},"ingredient/whole-wheat-flour":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/whole-wheat-flour","n":"Whole-wheat flour","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Wheat flour is a powder made from the grinding of common wheat used for human consumption. Wheat varieties are called \"soft\" or \"weak\" if gluten content is low, and are called \"hard\" or \"strong\" if they have high gluten content. Hard flour, or bread flour, is high in gluten, with 12% to 14% gluten content, and its dough has elastic toughness that holds its shape well once baked. Soft flour is comparatively low in gluten and thus results in a loaf with a finer, crumbly texture. Soft flour, in the US, is usually divided into cake flour, which is the lowest in gluten, and pastry flour, which has slightly more gluten than cake flour."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-whole-wheat-flour"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Whole-wheat_flour"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine",[["Ciabatta","dish/ciabatta"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Fermentation starter","ingredient/fermentation-starter"],["Olive oil","ingredient/olive-oil"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Yeast","ingredient/yeast"]]}]},"ingredient/coconut-cream":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/coconut-cream","n":"Coconut cream","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 2 dishes across 3 cuisines","intro":[["Coconut cream is similar to coconut milk, but is thicker due to a higher concentration of coconut and a lower amount of water in a ratio of about 4:1. Coconut cream is used as an ingredient in cooking, and has an unsweetened flavor."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-coconut-cream"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coconut_cream"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":3,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Rendang","dish/rendang"]]],["Padang food","cuisine/padang-food",[["Rendang","dish/rendang"]]],["Vietnamese cuisine","cuisine/vietnamese-cuisine",[["Coconut candy","dish/coconut-candy"]]]]},{"h":"Where it's grown","grown":{"what":"Coconuts, in shell","years":"2020 to 2024","url":"https://www.fao.org/faostat/en/#data/QCL","world":64008332,"top":[["Indonesia","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",17785077],["Philippines","cuisine/filipino-cuisine",14706484],["India","cuisine/indian-cuisine",14098913],["Brazil","cuisine/brazilian-cuisine",2739588],["Sri Lanka","cuisine/sri-lankan-cuisine",2161810],["Vietnam","cuisine/vietnamese-cuisine",1984680],["Myanmar","cuisine/burmese-cuisine",1266842],["Papua New Guinea","cuisine/papua-new-guinean-cuisine",1199148],["Mexico","cuisine/mexican-cuisine",1097291],["Thailand","cuisine/thai-cuisine",895280],["Malaysia","cuisine/malaysian-cuisine",604575],["Ghana","cuisine/ghanaian-cuisine",514070],["Dominican Rep.",null,507367],["Tanzania","cuisine/tanzanian-cuisine",483456],["Bangladesh","cuisine/bangladeshi-cuisine",410093]]}},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Coconut milk","ingredient/coconut-milk"],["Coconut","ingredient/coconut"],["Malt","ingredient/malt"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Candlenut","ingredient/candlenut"],["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Garcinia atroviridis","ingredient/garcinia-atroviridis"],["Illicium verum","ingredient/illicium-verum"],["Lemongrass","ingredient/lemongrass"],["Turmeric","ingredient/turmeric"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/2/20/Coconut_cream_%28cropped%29.jpg","w":500,"h":500,"by":"wfairchild","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coconut_cream_(cropped).jpg"}},"ingredient/profiteroles":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/profiteroles","n":"Profiteroles","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 2 cuisines","intro":[["A profiterole, or chou à la crème (French: [ʃu a la kʁɛm]), known as a cream puff in the US and Canada, is a filled French choux pastry ball with a typically sweet and moist filling of whipped cream, custard, pastry cream, or ice cream. They may be garnished with chocolate sauce, caramel, or a dusting of powdered sugar."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-profiteroles"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Profiteroles"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["French cuisine","cuisine/french-cuisine",[["Croquembouche","dish/croquembouche"]]],["New Caledonian cuisine","cuisine/new-caledonian-cuisine",[["Croquembouche","dish/croquembouche"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Caramel","ingredient/caramel"],["Profiterole","ingredient/profiterole"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/7/75/Paris_2e_-_Rue_Vivienne_-_Restaurant_Le_Vaudeville_-_Profiteroles_au_chocolat.jpg/500px-Paris_2e_-_Rue_Vivienne_-_Restaurant_Le_Vaudeville_-_Profiteroles_au_chocolat.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Romainbehar","lic":"CC0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/deed.en","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paris_2e_-_Rue_Vivienne_-_Restaurant_Le_Vaudeville_-_Profiteroles_au_chocolat.jpg"}},"ingredient/tapioca-pearl":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/tapioca-pearl","n":"Tapioca pearl","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["A tapioca pearl, also known as a tapioca ball, is an edible translucent sphere produced from tapioca, a starch made from the cassava root. They originated as a cheaper alternative to sago in Southeast Asian cuisine. When used as an ingredient in bubble tea, they are most commonly referred to as pearls or boba. The starch pearls are typically five to ten millimeters (0.2 to 0.4 inches) in diameter. By adding different ingredients, like water, sugar, or some other type of sweetener like honey, tapioca pearls can be made to vary in color and in texture. Various forms of tapioca pearls include black, clear, flavored, popping, and mini. Tapioca pearls are commonly soaked in sugar syrup to make them sweet and chewy. In teas, they are often added for their texture, with the flavor being provided by the drink itself."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-tapioca-pearl"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tapioca_pearl"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Es doger","dish/es-doger"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Avocado","